<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin-bottom: 10px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Exploring Our Global Community – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Exploring Our Global Community</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> What Can I Learn From Other Cultures?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Comprehend lectures on general academic subjects. Interactive: Engage in debates on change, including local or global changes.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can comprehend main ideas in academic lectures or presentations about global travel and tourism (e.g., "The speaker explained that Machu Picchu is one of the most famous ancient sites in the world, but Chichen Itza is larger and has more well-preserved structures.")
            Can participate in debates on global cultural issues, recognizing and responding to different opinions (e.g., "Some people believe traveling sustainably is expensive, while others argue that it benefits local economies in the long run.")
            Can ask questions about global changes discussed in debates (e.g., "What ideas were the speakers sharing about how different cultures were managing tourism impacts?" During the debate, community leaders were explaining how they were balancing tourism with preserving their cultural heritage and natural environments.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2; border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images featuring diverse global destinations (e.g., "pyramids" in Egypt, "beaches" in Thailand, "mountains" in Peru, a "waterfall" in Brazil, traditional markets). Students discuss in pairs: "Which of these places would you dream of visiting? What kind of 'experience' do you think you would gain there?" The teacher introduces key vocabulary words like "breathtaking," "diversity," "adventure," and "destination."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher pronounces the new vocabulary words clearly, modeling the stress patterns for multisyllabic words like "adVENture" and "des-ti-NA-tion," and the pronunciation of "pyramids." The teacher then models the second conditional: "If I could travel anywhere in the 'world,' I 'would' visit the pyramids in Egypt because I 'would' love to 'explore' ancient history." The teacher explicitly demonstrates the vowel reduction in "could" (/kəd/ or /kʊd/) and "would" (/wəd/ or /wʊd/) in the sentence: "If I could TRAVEL aROUND the WORLD, I would VISIT EGYPT."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say a view is 'breathtaking,' does that mean it's ugly or very beautiful and impressive?" (Expected answer: Very beautiful/impressive)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When we talk about 'cultural diversity,' are we discussing one type of culture or many different types?" (Expected answer: Many different types)</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Exploring Our Global Community."</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An authentic audio clip (2-3 minutes) from a travel podcast titled "Unforgettable Journeys: Learning from 'Different' Cultures," featuring two travelers sharing their personal "experiences" and observations about "cultural diversity" in "remote" "destinations." The register is informal but informative, focusing on narrative excerpts and descriptions. The audio will incorporate the target vocabulary (e.g., "adventure," "unforgettable," "enriching," "traditions") and grammar (superlatives, past continuous).</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The learner comprehends the main ideas and specific details in short lectures or presentations on general academic subjects, including global travel and cultural issues.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question (First Listen):</b> "What is the main topic of the podcast episode?" (A: Learning from different cultures through travel experiences.)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Second Listen):</b> Students listen again and mark statements as True or False based on specific details mentioned.</li>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The speakers were mostly discussing 'romantic' 'destinations' in Europe." (False)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One speaker described an 'enriching' 'experience' observing local 'traditions' in a 'remote' village." (True)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"They mentioned that an 'Exchange Program' can 'enhance' one's 'understanding' of global 'perspectives'." (True)</li>
                </ul>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening for specific vocabulary (e.g., "adventure," "unforgettable," "explore," "understanding," "diversity"), grammar (second conditional, superlatives, past continuous), and pronunciation (stressed/unstressed syllables, vowel reduction). Pragmatic: showing interest in shared experiences.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Listening for Gap-fill):</b> Students receive a transcript excerpt from the podcast with 5-7 key vocabulary words or grammatical structures (e.g., "superlatives," "past continuous") removed. They listen to the audio segment again (or a new, similar short clip) and fill in the blanks. Example: "While I 'was exploring' the market, I saw 'the most beautiful' traditional crafts."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice (Identifying Grammar and Pronunciation):</b> The teacher plays specific sentences from the audio focusing on the second conditional ("If I could 'travel' around the 'world,' I 'would' visit the Andes 'mountains'") and superlatives ("It was 'the most magnificent waterfall' I'd ever seen"). Students identify and write down the modal verbs with vowel reduction (could/would) and superlative adjectives. They also identify multisyllabic words and mark their stressed syllables.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction (Listening for Details in Hypothetical Narratives):</b> Students work in pairs. Student A narrates a hypothetical travel "adventure" using the second conditional, incorporating at least three new vocabulary words (e.g., "If I could visit a 'remote' 'destination,' I 'would explore' the 'cultural diversity' of a small Amazonian community."). Student B listens attentively and notes down: 1) the 'dream' destination, 2) three new vocabulary words used, and 3) one thing Student A 'would' learn or 'experience'. After Student A finishes, Student B asks, "What is one 'tradition' you found interesting while you 'were traveling' (hypothetically)?" or "How 'would' your 'experience' 'broaden' your 'perspectives'?" They then switch roles.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Exploring Our Global Community."</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Listening Comprehension & Question Formulation. Students will listen to a short audio segment (approx. 2 minutes) from a panel discussion or a mini-lecture about global changes related to tourism or cultural exchange programs. This audio will present different "perspectives" on challenges and "benefits," potentially using past continuous to 'narrate' past developments and superlatives to 'compare' approaches.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen to the audio once to grasp the main ideas and identify the different opinions presented regarding the impacts of tourism or "Exchange Programs." (Learning Outcome: Can comprehend main ideas in academic lectures).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They listen a second time, specifically noting down key arguments, facts, or examples shared by the speakers.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">In small groups (3-4 students), they collaboratively formulate 2-3 specific, respectful questions to ask the speakers if they were participating in the debate. These questions should demonstrate understanding of the audio content and seek further clarification or alternative "perspectives" on the "global" changes discussed. (Learning Outcome: Can ask questions about global changes discussed in debates; Pragmatic: Respectfully discussing cultural differences and travel experiences; Sociolinguistic: Lead group projects ensuring everyone has input). Example question: "What ideas were the speakers sharing about how different cultures were managing tourism impacts?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group then presents one of their questions to the class. The teacher facilitates a brief discussion based on the questions, ensuring students maintain a listening focus and engage with the content.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group work and question formulation, and a brief listening quiz.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listening Quiz:</b> Students complete a short multiple-choice or true/false quiz based on the main ideas and specific details from the audio used in Stage 4.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Question Evaluation:</b> The teacher collects the groups' formulated questions and assesses their relevance, clarity, and reflection of listening comprehension.</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listening Comprehension:</b> Accuracy in answering quiz questions about the audio's main ideas and details.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Integration of vocabulary words (e.g., "diversity," "perspectives," "benefits," "understanding") in formulated questions.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity & Relevance:</b> Questions are clear, grammatically sound, and directly related to the audio content.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Active participation and contribution to group discussion during question formulation (observed).</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new vocabulary word you learned today that helps you talk about 'global' 'travel' or 'culture'?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How well do you feel you understood the main ideas from today's listening activities? (1-5 scale)"</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most interesting fact or 'perspective' you heard about 'other cultures' today, and why?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one 'skill' related to 'listening' that you want to 'develop' further in our next lesson?"</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson, particularly regarding student engagement in listening activities and vocabulary acquisition? The explicit pronunciation practice for modal verbs and multisyllabic words seemed effective in drawing attention to phonological features. The variety of listening tasks kept students focused.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds might be needed for the next lesson (e.g., more targeted practice on specific grammar points, additional visual aids, or different grouping strategies)? Some students struggled with the second conditional in spontaneous speech; perhaps a quick review or a short grammar focus activity might be beneficial. I will ensure the audio for Lesson 2 is clearly aligned with the vocabulary introduced today.</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 2, students will 'read' a text that uses the same core vocabulary and grammar structures presented and practiced in this listening lesson. The reading will provide deeper context and reinforce the 'understanding' of "Exploring Our Global Community" and "What Can I Learn From Other Cultures?"</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>